### source pull [exit 0]
Update the document from its sources: add operations and bindings for new
targets, update changed ones, and report bindings whose target is gone. With
no sources named, pull every source. Uses a handler for each source's kind.
ob status shows the same changes without making them.

--target binds one target of one source, as ob source inspect lists it:
ob adds a binding for it, and the suggested operation if the document does
not have it yet. Nothing else changes.

Changes <obi> in place. Give - as <obi> to read a document from stdin and
print the result instead, or use --dry-run to see the change without
writing it.

Usage:
  ob source pull <obi> [<source>...] [flags]

Examples:
  ob source pull tasks.obi.json
  ob source pull tasks.obi.json httpApi --dry-run
  ob source pull tasks.obi.json httpApi --target "POST /tasks/{id}/archive"

Flags:
      --dry-run         show the change without writing it
  -h, --help            help for pull
      --target string   bind this one target of the source, as ob source inspect lists it


### binding add [exit 0]
Add a binding: a way to carry out an operation through a source. --content
is whatever the source's kind needs, such as which endpoint or tool to call.
An operation can have several bindings, even through the same source.

--idempotent claims that repeating a call through this binding adds no
further intended effects (it says nothing about retry safety on its own);
--idempotent=false claims it can; leaving it out claims nothing.
--preference records the author's preference among the operation's
bindings; ob invoke shows it but does not choose by it.

Changes <obi> in place. Give - as <obi> to read a document from stdin and
print the result instead, or use --dry-run to see the change without
writing it.

Usage:
  ob binding add <obi> <name> --operation <operation> --source <source> [flags]

Examples:
  ob binding add tasks.obi.json completeTask.mcp --operation completeTask --source mcpServer \
      --content '{"target":"tools/complete_task"}' --idempotent

Flags:
      --content string       whatever the source's kind reads, such as which target to call: JSON, @file, or -
      --deprecated           mark deprecated; --deprecated=false marks it current
      --description string   a human-readable description
      --dry-run              show the change without writing it
  -h, --help                 help for add
      --idempotent           claim that repeating a call adds no further intended effects; --idempotent=false claims it can
      --operation string     the operation it carries out (name or alias)
      --preference int       the author's preference among this operation's bindings (an integer; higher is stronger)
      --source string        the source it goes through


